  "summary": "An ancient building technique using compacted earth as structural walls with distinctive layered texture.",
  "description": "Characterized by distinctive horizontal striations from successive layers of compacted earth mixture, often with visible aggregate. Practiced for millennia across arid regions, experiencing modern revival as a sustainable building material with thermal mass benefits and organic aesthetic.",
  "culturalContext": "Evidence of ancient use of rammed earth has been found in Neolithic archaeological sites such as those of the Fertile Crescent, dating to the 9th–7th millennium BC, and of the Yangshao and Longshan cultures in China, dating to 5000 BCE. By 2000 BCE, rammed-earth architectural techniques (夯土 Hāng tǔ) were commonly used for walls and foundations in China.",
