  "visualDNA": {
    "Overview": "The compressive strength of rammed earth is dictated by factors such as soil type, particle size distribution, amount of compaction, moisture content of the mix and the type and amount of stabiliser used. Well-produced cement-stabilised rammed-earth walls can be anywhere between 5 and 18 MPa (700 and 3,000 psi). Higher compressive strength may require more cement. But addition of more cement can affect the permeability of the walls. Properly constructed rammed earth endures for thousands of years, as many ancient structures that are still standing around the world demonstrate."
  },
